   "cite": "activerecord-5.1.7/lib/active_record/migration/compatibility.rb:25 and :31 -- `class V5_0 < V5_1 ... def create_table(table_name, options = {}); if adapter_name == \"PostgreSQL\"` and `unless adapter_name == \"Mysql2\" && options[:id] == :bigint`, both of them a bare `adapter_name` sent to the migration object itself. The same two tests in activerecord-5.2.8.1/lib/active_record/migration/compatibility.rb:53 and :59 read `connection.adapter_name`. What makes the difference reach the log is `ActiveRecord::Migration#method_missing` (migration.rb:835-838 in 5.1.7, :857-860 in 5.2.8.1, unchanged between them): a message the migration does not define is forwarded to the connection inside `say_with_time \"#{method}(#{arg_list})\"`, so 5.1's bare call announces itself as `adapter_name()` and 5.2's explicit receiver goes straight to the connection and announces nothing. `V4_2 < V5_0` (compatibility.rb:89 in 5.1.7, :117 in 5.2.8.1) is the version these eleven migrations declare, so every one of them runs that branch. Neither release notes this in its CHANGELOG; the framework source is the whole of the documentation, and it is unambiguous.",
